\f0\fs24 \cf2 <$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Agent Jones stood in the balmy night air of a Christmas pre-dawn, and watched from the southern perimeter of the car park as a PAPER commando team spread out across the office park forecourt then secured the Traverse station entrance. He adjusted his black tie with satisfaction as Jihad Man strolled the forecourt and joined the squad.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 Agent Jones was the best kind of angry, and allowed himself the pleasure of reciting, \'93I am not human. I am the biological manifestation of the Company\'92s will. In the name of stability I destabilise. In the name of order I deliver chaos
\f2\i .\'94
\f1\i0 \
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 It all connected. From Travis Teh to Sarah Jacobson to Anthony Chantara and the Elvi to Eloise Shakti to the disc. \
Jihad Man was up to his neck in it. Agent Jones had hacked his phone, and had been monitoring his communications and whereabouts for days. He knew of Dunhilton\'92s plans to attack the Shakti compound in the mountains before Major Midler who, Jones was sure, was unaware of Jihad Man\'92s alternative operation to smuggle personnel and arms up from the valley floor. As far as Jones could piece the intelligence together, given all of Jihad Man\'92s feints and counter-feints, the objective of PAPER\'92s infiltration of Sublime Point was to rescue Eloise Shakti even while the UDF was campaigning to eliminate her. But then Midler stood the UDF down, so it seemed Midler\'92s was the last counter-feint of all\'97a distraction while Jihad Man deployed his own commando squad.\
When they disappeared down the station ramp, Agent Jones ran the perimeter of the forecourt to follow. At the top of the ramp he could hear them descending as quiet as they thought sufficient. He waited for the sounds of elevators arriving. There was a sound of a heavy door shutting quietly in the acoustically live space of the near-empty concourse. Agent Jones stole to a corridor next to the elevators and listened. He followed green 
\fs20 EXIT
\fs22  signs to a door, opened it a little and heard quiet footsteps echoing down a concrete stair well.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Gerry found the ruined Traverse and, like Chanta\'92s party around eight minutes earlier, found the access road, the service bay and sufficient trucks to transport twice the number of his team and bowling club on a wine tour. Gerry ordered the tyres of all but one of the vehicles to be deflated.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 At the other end of the road, Gerry\'92s team found themselves in a similarly sized service bay. There were several exits, none of which were labelled with any words, icons, codes, runes or arcane sigils that might provide guidance or counsel about where they would lead. They took the one they guessed would be the most direct route back to the Traverse terminus. They emerged onto a concourse finished as though Eloise expected to be met there. The air was cool and scented like rainforest. The ceiling rose in arcs of honey and chocolate sandstone layers, above a floor of cream travertine, crossed by broad curving paths of flamed green monzonite that led from the platform towards exits. Smoked-oak benches curved around beds of tree ferns beneath concentrations of artificial daylight. Water ran down bronze walls into shallow pools of tropical fish. Gerry was tempted to stick his finger in a pool to test the temperature then thought better of it; knowing Eloise, some of the fish species in there could chew your arm off.\
Gerry expected a statue, made of white marble, dressed in gold leaf, and standing more than four little old ladies tall.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 There were wallabies. Gerry wondered about the welfare of the hoppers, perhaps more than anyone whose career he\'92d destroyed.\
There was not a directory anywhere, not a map, not a 
\fs20 YOU ARE HERE
\fs22  guide. There were no 
\fs20 EXIT
\fs22  signs to exit by. Of course, Gerry didn\'92t expect a 
\fs20 THIS WAY TO NEUTRALISE THE BOSS
\fs22  or 
\fs20 VIRAL LABS AHEAD\'97BREATHE AT YOUR OWN RISK
\fs22  sign, he\'92d hoped there\'92d be directions to named wings or staff facilities that might give him a few clues. Nothing. Eloise\'92s nail polish was all over this: 
\f2\i if you\'92ve come this far, you\'92re expected to know where you are going.
\f1\i0 \
Fortunately, the place was empty, like the whole place had been locked down and everyone had either evacuated or were in well-advised hiding. Gerry sent scout parties with orders to be back in eight minutes.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Agent Jones followed the sounds, as quiet and professional as Gerry\'92s team was. He could hear the trucks down the tunnel as he followed in a golf cart; he could easily trace them through from the service bay at the Ruined Castle end of the Traverse, and was again able to lurk in a service corridor, just out of sight of Jihad Man and his squad in the concourse.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 He did, however, manage to spook a couple of wallabies; bounded off the soft ground laid especially for them onto the stone floor, and skated on their clawed feet. Heads in the squad turned, then thought no more of it.\
The concourse became silent. Agent Jones risked peering around the corner. Jihad Man appeared to be alone. Tactical error\'97Agent Jones would have advised against it.\
Agent Jones drew his pistol, made aim, and stepped out from the corridor. Once he had Jihad Man in his gun sight, he advanced with the stealth and ease of a trained killer, keeping as much as possible at his target\'92s back. He could have just shot him.\
\'93Jihad Man!\'94\
His quarry turned, and kept his hands where they could be seen. \'93Dunhilton sent you,\'94 he said.\
Agent Jones could hear the uncertainty in his voice. \'93You will ask no questions.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93What do you want?\'94 Jihad Man asked.\
\'93It all connects.\'94 \
\'93What connects?\'94 \
\'93The disc. I must retrieve the disc. It is here.\'94\
\'93The disc?\'94 Jihad Man was pleading ignorance.\
Agent Jones snarled. \'93My mission is to retrieve the disc. Eloise Shakti has the disc. I will retrieve the disc. You will not retrieve the disc. You will not exfiltrate Eloise Shakti.\'94\
\'93Exfiltrate?\'94 Jihad Man laughed right in Agent Jones\'92 face. \'93My orders are to neutralise Eloise Shakti.\'94\
Agent Jones set his jaw. \'93It all connects. Jacobson and Chanta stole the disc and gave it to Shakti. And you have your promotions.\'94\
\'93Dunhilton sent you.\'94 Jihad Man was surer of the lie now. He was a fool.\
\'93You sent the UDF as a cover for your operation\'85\'94\
Jihad Man laughed. \'93Well, you got that right.\'94\
\'93\'85so you could exfiltrate Shakti under the confusion.\'94\
\'93And you got that all wrong, mate. We\'92re on the same side. We\'92re here to bring Shakti down.\'94\
\'93I am here to bring you down.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 If Gerry kept the black-tie Jones talking, the man stalking the clone from behind would take him out. At least that was Gerry\'92s assessment; the man had put his finger to his lips\'97
\f2\i quiet
\f1\i0 \'97and drawn a knife. And Gerry\'92s own reconn parties would be back in minutes. Gerry needed to process how the knife-wielding stalker had come to be there at all, but he filed that away for later.\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
<!$Scr_Ps::0><$Scr_Ps::1>
\f1\fs22 The black-tie Jones clone had just threatened to bring him down, then said, \'93You will confess.\'94\
\pard\pardeftab720\fi360\sl220\qj\partightenfactor0
\cf2 \'93To what, exactly?\'94\
\'93To your conspiracy,\'94 the Jones clone said.\
\'93Which one?\'94 Gerry laughed at 
\f2\i conspiracy\'97
\f1\i0 typical Jones clone behaviour, to find conspiracies everywhere. He never trusted them, and he\'92d outlaw the lot of them when he became CEO.\
That froze the clone\'92s circuits. Gerry could almost read him trying to decide where to start.\
\'93You ordered the excavation beneath the Stickett Inn,\'94 Jones said.\
\'93Yes.\'94\
The knife-wielder approached calmly; Gerry resisted the impulse to watch him. Two more steps; they were fast and precise, as was the grapple to expose the clone\'92s throat, as was the clean stroke of the blade. The clone\'92s pistol fired into the ceiling as he fell. The clone\'92s assassin relieved him of the gun and laid him on his back, where he exsanguinated like a slaughtered goat.\
Water still fell and aerated the pools for the tropical fish.\
\pard\pardeftab720\sl220\sb220\qj\partightenfactor0

\f0\fs24 \cf2 \kerning1\expnd0\expndtw0 <!$Scr_Ps::1><$Scr_Ps::0>
\f1\fs22 Halo wiped his knife on the sleeve of the Jones clone\'92s black jacket. He had to really look to see the blood glisten. \expnd0\expndtw0\kerning0
